# Backend Python

Ruff at 100 columns, Python 3.11, `from __future__ import annotations` at the top of every
module. `E`, `F`, `I`, `UP`, `B`, `W` are selected; keep imports absolute (`from finops.… import`).

## Numbers

- No literal rate, price, or dollar amount outside `demo.py`. Rates come from `PricingClient`.
- `None` means not measured and must survive as `None`. Do not write `or 0.0`, `get(key, 0)`, or
  `float(value or 0)` in a code path that feeds a cost, a saving, or an attribution — a zero
  there reads on the dashboard as "free" rather than as "unknown".
- Round only at the boundary where the value is stored or displayed, never mid-calculation.
- Constants get a name and a comment saying why that value, in the module that uses them.
  Anything that decides whether a rule fires belongs in `config.Thresholds` instead.

## Errors

- Catch `(ClientError, BotoCoreError)` and only around the call that can fail. A broad `except`
  that returns an empty list turns a denied permission into an empty service.
- Failures a user should know about become capability notes; failures that are merely noisy go to
  `logger.debug` with the identifier that failed.
- `logger`, never `print`, outside `cli.py`'s own output.
- `# noqa: BLE001` is acceptable only where isolating one component's failure is the point, and
  carries a comment saying so.

## Types and shape

- Annotate every signature; prefer precise types and `X | None` over `Optional`, `Any` only for
  genuinely untyped AWS payloads.
- Pydantic models in `model.py`, dataclasses for internal context objects.
- Module docstrings say what the module is for and what is easy to get wrong about the service it
  covers. Public helpers get a one-line docstring; private ones get one when the name is not
  enough.

## Comments

A comment explains a constraint, a trade-off, or a fact about AWS the code cannot state — why a
threshold is what it is, why an API is called twice, why a value is excluded. Never narrate what
the next line does, and never explain a change to a reviewer.
